sections.body:
**First users (proposed pilot candidates):**
- agricultural advisory and extension teams;
- regional water-planning groups;
- universities and training institutions teaching water balance and climate risk.

**Why they would care:** they already combine weather, crop-water and storage data by hand. BlueSense puts these in one place, with the source and date kept beside each value.

> **Honest status:** no TAM, customer count, interviews or procurement approval exist yet. These segments are hypotheses to test in a pilot. Some data licences (FAO AQUASTAT CC BY-NC-SA, the CARTO free tier) restrict commercial use, and a commercial version would need other sources.

Real, verifiable progress (no users or revenue are claimed):

- **Live on Vercel:** the frontend plus the backend. The AI Copilot answers on the shared link.
- **Documentation RAG is live:** on the website, "How is the forecast made?" cited `MODEL_CARD.md > Models`.
- **Team-built MCP server** (`bluesense-mcp`, 15 tools), used from Claude Code in VS Code and from the Claude Desktop app.
- **n8n daily digest:** published. A test run on 20 Nov 2026 succeeded and the email arrived.
- **Engineering depth:**
  - 274 backend test functions, 65 Vitest cases and 79 Playwright cases in source;
  - 45 API operations;
  - 25 database tables with checksummed migrations.
- **Data coverage:**
  - ERA5 daily weather for 8 divisions from 1991 to Nov 2026 (104,744 rows);
  - GRACE storage for 259 months;
  - 25 AQUASTAT indicators.

Today, planners use the original **source portals** (FAO AQUASTAT, Open-Meteo/ERA5, GRACE data centres), **spreadsheets**, and **specialist modelling tools**.

How BlueSense differs, as a design choice and not a benchmark result:
- **one place** for weather, statistics, storage, forecasts and scenarios;
- the **source, date and data kind on every number**, so an old statistic never looks current;
- **AI that cannot invent numbers:** read-only tools, cited documentation and a number check.

> No head-to-head comparison with other products has been run yet. A side-by-side exercise is part of the proposed pilot.

**Predict water risk, protect every drop.**

BlueSense aims to become the trusted, transparent water-intelligence layer for Bangladesh. It would follow the chain **Predict → Analyze → Visualize → Optimize → Recommend** and keep it honest about what each number is and where it comes from.

Next milestones:
- an advisory-team pilot;
- observed BWDB station data;
- sharper forecasts (seasonal climate inputs, calibrated intervals);
- a self-refreshing live deployment.

**What it does:** BlueSense combines public weather reanalysis and forecasts, FAO AQUASTAT statistics, GRACE satellite water storage and administrative boundaries in one web workspace. It adds ML forecasts, deterministic engines, rule-based monitoring and a grounded AI Copilot.

**Geography:** Bangladesh, its 8 divisions (one representative weather point each) and 64 districts. Districts are map reference boundaries only.

**Target users:** agricultural extension advisers, regional water planners and educators (proposed).

**Core use cases:**
- see current and recent conditions;
- forecast the water balance 1–6 months ahead;
- test scenarios;
- balance and optimise a water budget;
- plan irrigation;
- ask grounded questions in English or Bangla.

**APIs exposed:** the BlueSense REST API under `/api` (45 operations, OpenAPI at `/api/openapi.json`, interactive docs at `/api/docs`).
- Every number is a `Measure` with its unit, data kind, source, dates and freshness.
- Newer endpoints return `{data, meta}`.
- Errors are `{detail, code}`.

**Other interfaces:**
- the MCP server `bluesense-mcp` (stdio, 15 read-only tools);
- the Copilot stream `POST /api/copilot/stream` (server-sent events).

**APIs used:** Open-Meteo (forecast, ERA5 archive, GloFAS flood), FAO AQUASTAT, CSR GRACE/GRACE-FO, Google Gemini (chat and embeddings); map tiles from CARTO/OpenStreetMap, AWS Terrain and JRC Global Surface Water.

**Auth model:**
- data endpoints are public (no login needed);
- admin sync uses a bearer token;
- the Copilot has per-IP, per-client and site-wide limits;
- there are no user accounts.

**Forecasting (ML):**
- Random Forest per horizon (1/3/6 months) on 53 lag and climatology features, with a chronological split (train ≤ 2016, validation 2017–2020, test 2021+);
- 1-month test RMSE **118.5 mm**, against 125.8 mm for climatology and 171.1 mm for persistence;
- 90% intervals from validation residuals (test coverage 84%);
- SHAP explanations.

**AI Copilot (LLM):**
- Gemini with 15 read-only JSON-schema tools that call the same functions as the UI;
- answers are streamed;
- numbers not found in the tool results are flagged;
- no answers without a key.

**RAG:**
- `search_docs` searches 5 project documents (Methods, Data sources, Model card, Limitations, Assumptions), cut into 32 section chunks;
- each chunk is prefixed with its title and section (contextual chunks);
- vectors are `gemini-embedding-2` (768 dimensions);
- hybrid score 0.6 cosine + 0.4 BM25, top 4, with keyword fallback;
- answers cite `[DOC > section]`;
- no GraphRAG.

**Personalisation:** none beyond the selected region and the user's own preferences in the browser.

**Explainability:** SHAP, a model card, a "why" list for irrigation advice, and data-kind and source badges.

**Short term (weeks):**
- restore the optional packages (h5py, XGBoost, the Anthropic SDK);
- correct stale documents;
- a retrieval evaluation set for RAG;
- run the advisory pilot.

**Mid term (months):**
- a self-refreshing deployment with persistent storage and scheduled sync;
- BWDB observed station data;
- a JPL GRACE adapter;
- seasonal-forecast inputs and calibrated (quantile) intervals.

**Long term:**
- several grid points per division;
- district-level hydrology;
- user accounts and roles for institutions;
- a remote MCP transport for partners.

- **Auth:** there are no user accounts. Admin data sync needs a bearer token (constant-time check). This `/docs` admin uses role passwords (Super Admin, Admin) and signed, HttpOnly session cookies.
- **RBAC:** `/docs` has two roles. The app itself has no user roles yet (planned).
- **API hardening:**
  - strict CORS;
  - Pydantic validation with ranges and patterns;
  - parameterised SQL;
  - read-only database connections;
  - sanitised errors.
- **AI guardrails:**
  - read-only tool allowlist with validated arguments;
  - user text, history and tool output treated as data;
  - output number check;
  - usage limits;
  - a provider data-use disclosure.
- **Secrets:** server-side only, never in the browser bundle or the repository.
- **Data protection:** checksummed migrations that reject destructive SQL, verified backups, and a checksum-verified, read-only live database.

BlueSense has **no user tracking or product analytics**. These operational signals do exist, and some are shown live above:

- data-source freshness and the last successful sync per source;
- Copilot model calls used today against the site-wide daily limit;
- open alerts by severity;
- model quality: test RMSE, interval coverage and dryness-class macro-F1;
- n8n run history (in n8n Cloud).
